Answers:

1. If Power BI is the only frontend supporting Redshift, why not use Athena and save money?

First, clarify the incorrect assumption.

Both Amazon Redshift and Amazon Athena can connect to Microsoft Power BI.

But they serve different workloads.

Redshift
	•	Fully managed data warehouse
	•	Optimized for BI workloads
	•	Supports:
	•	materialized views
	•	stored procedures
	•	workload management
	•	row/column security
	•	high concurrency

Transformations in Redshift
	•	Complex joins
	•	Aggregations
	•	Window functions
	•	Stored procedures
	•	ELT transformations

Example:

Sales fact table joined with 10 dimension tables
Daily aggregates
Ranking customers by region

Athena

Serverless SQL engine on Amazon S3.

You pay per query scanned.

Good for:
	•	ad hoc queries
	•	data lake exploration

Limitations:
	•	slower for complex BI queries
	•	limited concurrency
	•	no native indexing
	•	weaker governance

Transformations in Athena

Mostly:
	•	simple SQL
	•	filtering
	•	basic aggregation

Example:

SELECT country, SUM(revenue)
FROM sales
GROUP BY country

Architect Answer

Use Athena for exploration, Redshift for production BI dashboards.

2. How much data is in Athena vs Redshift?

Athena

Data lives in S3

Can query PB scale easily.

Example:

1 PB clickstream logs in S3
Athena queries directly

But performance degrades with complex joins.

Redshift

Typical size:
	•	TB → PB scale

But data must be loaded into cluster storage.

Example:

10 TB curated analytics layer
Loaded into Redshift

Architect pattern:

Raw Data → S3 → Athena
Curated BI → Redshift

3. Iceberg / Hudi / Delta tables – do they give Athena advanced capabilities?

Yes.

These are open table formats for data lakes.

Examples:
	•	Apache Iceberg
	•	Apache Hudi
	•	Delta Lake

They add warehouse-like capabilities to data lakes.

Capabilities

Feature	Data lake	Iceberg/Hudi/Delta
Update	❌	✅
Delete	❌	✅
ACID	❌	✅
Time travel	❌	✅
Schema evolution	❌	✅

Example:

UPDATE customers
SET address='Mumbai'
WHERE id=100

Athena alone cannot do this.

But Athena + Iceberg can.

Still, query performance and concurrency are better in Redshift.

4. Redshift cluster – which type?

Architect should mention node types.

Node Types

RA3 nodes (modern)

Recommended.

Benefits:
	•	compute/storage separation
	•	managed storage
	•	scale independently

Example:

ra3.4xlarge
ra3.16xlarge

DC2 nodes

Older generation.

High performance but fixed storage.

Architect answer:

	We use RA3 clusters because they allow separation of compute and storage and reduce cost for large datasets.

